<article class="tm-article">
  <nav class="tm-breadcrumb"><a href="/hc/en-us">Help Center</a> / How biogas is produced</nav>

  <p>Biogas production is a circular cycle. It starts and ends in agriculture: residual products become energy, and the energy's residue becomes fertiliser. Here is what the journey looks like at a modern biogas plant.</p>

  <ol class="tm-steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Collecting the biomass</h3>
      <p>Livestock manure, straw, food waste and organic residual products from the food industry are transported to the plant. Nothing goes to waste – it is material that would otherwise be discarded.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Digestion without oxygen</h3>
      <p>The biomass is pumped into large, sealed tanks – reactors – where it is heated. Without oxygen, micro-organisms break down the material and form a methane-rich raw gas. The process typically takes two to three weeks.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Upgrading to biomethane</h3>
      <p>The raw gas is cleaned of CO₂ and impurities so it reaches the same quality as natural gas. It is now called biomethane and can be used in everything from gas stoves to industry.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Out onto the gas grid</h3>
      <p>The finished biomethane is fed directly into the existing Danish gas grid – or liquefied as BioLNG for transport.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Fertiliser back to the field</h3>
      <p>What remains after digestion – the digested biomass – is a nutrient-rich, low-odour fertiliser product that is returned to farmers and replaces synthetic fertiliser.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tm-callout">
    <strong>A closed loop:</strong> The same manure that becomes green energy ends up as fertiliser on the field again. This reduces both methane emissions and the need for fossil-based synthetic fertiliser.
  </div>

  <h2>Why is it efficient?</h2>
  <p>By using waste that already exists, biogas creates energy without taking up new farmland. At the same time, a large share of the methane that would otherwise have escaped into the atmosphere from manure storage is removed. That makes biogas one of the most climate-efficient green fuels we have today.</p>

